{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3180,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physics progresses by breaking our intuitions,\nbut we are now at a point where further progress"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9060,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may require us to do away with the most intuitive\nand seemingly fundamental concepts of all—space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15840,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and time themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23000,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24000,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "came into its modern form as a description\nof how objects move through space and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28440,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are the stage on which physics plays\nout."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32460,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that stage begins to fall apart on the\ntiniest scales and the largest energies, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38400,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics falls apart with it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40260,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many believe that the only way to make physics\nwhole again is to break what may be our most"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46380,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "powerful intuition yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48360,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our minds, space and time \nseem pretty fundamental, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51960,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but that primacy may not extend beyond our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55500,
    "dDurationMs": 420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In many of the new theories that are pushing\nthe edge of physics, spacetime at its elementary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61680,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "level is not what we think it is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re going to explore the “realness”\nof space and time over a few upcoming episodes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71400,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll ask: Do our minds hold a faithful\nrepresentation of something real out there,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78840,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if not, why do we think about space and\ntime the way we do?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83580,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if space and time aren’t fundamental,\nwhat is?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88140,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What do space and time emerge from?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90720,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today we’re taking the first step by\nexploring how the notion of absolute space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96060,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and time in physics came about in the first\nplace, and how that notion is beginning to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101760,
    "dDurationMs": 540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fall apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102300,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have this sense of space as an extended\nemptiness - a volume waiting to be filled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108480,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with matter - a regular, continuous, mappable\n… space, in which everything that exists"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115080,
    "dDurationMs": 900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is embedded."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116580,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meanwhile time is the continuous rolling of\nfuture into past through the present, all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122460,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "governed by the same unstoppable clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124380,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this idea of space and time as having\nan existence “out there”, independent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130860,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of its contents, became cemented in popular\nintuition relatively recently, at the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136680,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time that they became cemented in physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139800,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However humans have been arguing over the\nreality or the fundamentalness of the dimensions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146100,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for millenia."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147420,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can summarise the two main conceptions\nof spacetime as either relational— space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152820,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a network of positional relationships of\nobjects —or absolute—a real entity that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160440,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exists independently of objects, and rather,\ncontains the objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164580,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The latter seems to have emerged only relatively\nrecently."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169380,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start with the ancients."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171780,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They certainly thought a lot about space—after\nall, they had maps and they invented geometry."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176940,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the geometries of Euclid and Pythagorus\nand others didn’t need the notion of space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183120,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as an absolute entity—they were relational."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185820,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, a triangle is defined by the\nrelative lengths of its sides and its internal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191100,
    "dDurationMs": 420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "angles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191520,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You don’t need a coordinate grid to define\na triangle—which is good, because the ancient"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197340,
    "dDurationMs": 1080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Greeks didn’t have one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199080,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sure, their maps had longitude and latitude,\nbut they didn’t have our own mathematical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204780,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "habit of gridding up empty space with x, y,\nand z axes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208860,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As such, they didn’t tend to think of empty\nspace as having its own independent existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214260,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea of the coordinate grid came much,\nmuch later."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218160,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps you’ve heard of the Cartesian coordinate\nsystem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221640,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "X, y, and z axes, each at 90 degrees to the\nothers and gridded up so that any point in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227160,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space can be defined with three numbers - the\nvalue of the closest grid-mark on each of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232500,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the axes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233700,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This idea feels pretty intuitive to many of\nus, but it wasn’t commonly used until after"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238800,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1637, when the French \nmathematician and philosopher "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242460,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rene Descartes made it cool."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245760,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With the coordinate system, it became possible\nto represent abstract numerical concepts in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250260,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spatial terms—for example, by graphing an\nalgebraic function."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255420,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it also gave us a tool for describing "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258000,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arbitrarily large and \nimaginary physical spaces—and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262440,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this application would soon revolutionise\nall of physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265980,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regarding the actual nature of space, Descartes\nwas firmly in the camp of philosophers like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271620,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Plato, who didn’t believe in empty space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274260,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Descartes said that space is only real as\nfar as it defines the extension of objects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280620,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282000,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the invention of the first true mathematical\ncoordinate system opened the door for a very,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287640,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very different conception of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289260,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that new conception was almost entirely\ndue to Isaac Newton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293760,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He gave us a set of equations that could,\napparently, completely describe the motion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299760,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of objects and how those motions change through\nthe forces of their interactions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304020,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Newtonian mechanics is built on Descartes’\ncoordinates, and assume a universal clock."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312180,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those mehcanics proved wildly \nsuccessful— revolutionary, really."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316560,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So much so that many, including Newton, began\nto see the foundational building blocks of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323580,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mechanics—the coordinate of space and\ntime—as in some way physically real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330360,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Newton himself insisted that space is absolute;\nit exists completely independently of any"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337320,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects within it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338280,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The empty volume implied by the Cartesian\ngrid is a thing in itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343740,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And according to Newton time is also absolute."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346920,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From Aristotle to Descartes, “time” was\nmostly understood as a counting of events."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353700,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But In Newton’s view, there’s a single\nuniversal clock that keeps the same time for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359820,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all observers--time passes “by itself ”, even\nin the absence of any change."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366480,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Newton also believed that there was an absolute\nnotion of stillness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370800,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like, a master frame of reference whose x,\ny, and z axes are unmoving, and if your position"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377460,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was fixed relative to those axes then you\nwere truly still."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381120,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is contrary to the ideas of Galileo a\ncentury prior, who showed us that velocity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386700,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is relative—the speed you measure for another\ntraveller depends on your own speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391680,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The laws of physics are the \nsame in any non-accelerating, "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395460,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or inertial frame, and so all such frames"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398460,
    "dDurationMs": 540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are equal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399000,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While Newton accepted the \nmathematical consequences "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402120,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Galilean relativity, he \nthought the difficulty we had"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406200,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in defining a preferred inertial frame was\na limitation of the human mind, not of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411780,
    "dDurationMs": 420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The success of Newtonian mechanics elevated\nthe notion of the realness of space and time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417960,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in everyone’s minds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419460,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there was one prominent naysayer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422520,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Newton had a nemesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424320,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or maybe it was Newton who was the nemesis\nto this guy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428460,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, he shared a mutually nemetical relationship "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433380,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the German mathematician \nGottfried Wilhelm Leibniz."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438060,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their most famous rivalry was over the discovery "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440520,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of calculus, which they \nfigured out independently—with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443940,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Leibniz probably getting to it first."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445500,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Newton however accused him of plagiarism, and being by far the most powerful scientist of his"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452460,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "day, secured the credit for himself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454620,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But another point of contention between these \ntwo was on the nature of space and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459420,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Leibniz did not accept Newton’s assertion\nthat these dimensions were in some sense real"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465720,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and independent of anything in them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468660,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, he thought that both space and time\nwere relational."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473580,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What does that even mean?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475140,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, it means that objects exist, but they\ndon’t live in a 3- or any other dimensional"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481920,
    "dDurationMs": 300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482220,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather, what we think of spatial separation\nis a quality of the objects themselves—or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488580,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather of the connection between them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490380,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Exactly why Leibnitz thought this and rejected \nNewton is a whole thing, that we don’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497820,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have time to get into right now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499980,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, let me try to give you a sense of\nwhat it could mean for space to be encoded"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506040,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in objects or in their relationships, rather\nthan existing independently to those objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511920,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s start by imagining only one dimension\nof space, represented as a line."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516600,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a Newtonian space, where every point\nrepresents an absolute position in a 1-D universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523560,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can put some particles in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526680,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The position of each in space is defined by\n- well, its position in space—whatever grid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532860,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mark it’s next to if we add a coordinate\nsystem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536520,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The particles might have intrinsic or internal\nproperties—say, mass, electric charge, etc.,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542640,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but their position isn’t a quantity that’s\nintrinsic to the particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548400,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Leibniz’s view there is no space, so\nwe get rid of the line."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553260,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The particles still exist, but they aren’t\nanywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556620,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re sort of just bundles of properties\nwith no size or location."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560100,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space doesn’t exist so maybe we should place\nthese particles on top of each other, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565980,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then again if location is meaningless we might\nas well separate them so we can see them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571260,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s add a new property to each particle\nthat we’ll call X."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575160,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "X is what we call a degree of freedom—something\nabout the particle that can take on different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580620,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "values, and it can change."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581940,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Other degrees of freedom could be energy and\nphase and spin and so on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587820,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "X behaves in a particular way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589560,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, it can change freely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593100,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If it’s changing, then it keeps changing\nat the same rate and in the same direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596640,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now these particles have no idea about each "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599640,
    "dDurationMs": 3809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other's existence, except \nin a special circumstance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603449,
    "dDurationMs": 8011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, If two particles have values of X that are\nclose to each other then those X values influence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611460,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other, changing the \nrate at which the dials turn."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe they want to try to be more similar,\nor maybe they try to be more different."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621300,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we were to represent these X values with\nposition on a number line - an x-axis - then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627900,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the behaviour of the particles looks just\nlike particles moving around in space and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632040,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attracting or repelling each other only when\nthey’re close together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636720,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can’t tell the difference between particles\nmoving in space versus space-like behaviour"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643620,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emerging from a degree of freedom within the\nparticles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647940,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This thought experiment isn’t explicitly "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650640,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what Leibniz described, \nnor is it how things should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654120,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really be to explain a universe like\nour own."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656400,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, we need 3 spatial dimensions,\nnot one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660000,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "X, Y, \u0026 Z would all have to be close to each\nother for particles to interact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665580,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, Leibnitz thought that position was \nencoded in the relationship between particles,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671640,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not in the objects themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673980,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He gave his elementary particles names - monads\n- which among other things had rudimentary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680940,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "consciousness, and that space emerged from\ntheir first-person perspectives of each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686640,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we don’t actually need \nthose extra qualities--the "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689880,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "idea of particles with interacting, internal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693060,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "degrees of freedom illustrates how space can\nemerge from the relationships between elements"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698460,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are themselves not in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700980,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that’s Leibnitz on space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703200,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He disagreed with Newton on time in a similar\nway, believing it to be a measure of the change"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intrinsic to each element, rather than a cosmic\nclock that kept the universe in sync."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course Newton was the undisputed boss of\nscience back then, and so his preference for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719160,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absolute space and time won over the physicists,\nand ultimately found its way into the popular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724380,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "imagination."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725940,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But who was really right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727320,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Are objects in space and moving through time,\nor are space and time somehow in objects and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734400,
    "dDurationMs": 780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their connections?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735180,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Are the dimensions absolute or relational?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738540,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The big next development seemed to support\nNewton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742560,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over the 19th century, our understanding of\nthe phenomena of electricity and magnetism"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747480,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "converged, revealing the existence of something\ncalled the electromagnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751380,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A field is just some property that can take\non a numerical value at all points in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756300,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, temperature is a field defined\nin the air around you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761700,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s emergent from the properties of the\nair particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765480,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the electromagnetic field doesn’t need\nparticles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768180,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the first time, it seemed that a field\ncould be a property of space itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, surely if space can have properties, then\nspace must objectively exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780060,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And more intrinsic properties emerged with\nthe development of quantum mechanics—for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784620,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example, space was shown to have a sort of\nenergy even in the absence of particles—so-called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790020,
    "dDurationMs": 900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vacuum energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790920,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, if we really want to decide whether\nspace and time are real—to judge between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796860,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Leibnitz and Newton—we need the ultimate\narbiter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800520,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We need the greatest expert of space and time\nthat ever lived—and that’s Albert Einstein."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806520,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve talked about Einstein’s special\nand general theories of relativity many times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810960,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812160,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s just go over what the theory changed\nabout our notions of the dimensions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817920,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With special relativity, the separation of\n3-D space and 1-D time ended."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823080,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They became 4-D spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825660,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein showed that our motion through space\nand our motion through time are linked."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832080,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A clock moving relative to you ticks slower\nfrom your perspective."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837060,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then with general relativity we see that\nthe presence of mass and energy stretch and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842100,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "warp both space and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843480,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This causes straight line trajectories that\nwe expect on a Cartesian grid to become curved,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850860,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the apparent change in an object’s path\nin the presence of mass is Einstein’s explanation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857880,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858840,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Relativity overturned some of Newton’s notions\nabout absolute space and time: that they are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864660,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "independent entities, that there’s a universal\nclock for time, and that there’s some sort"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 870000,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of ultimate, rigid coordinate system for space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873840,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what did these mean for the central question\nof this episode: what about the realness of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880380,
    "dDurationMs": 720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space and time?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882180,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, spacetime in Einstein’s universe\nkind of feels even more substantial than before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888060,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s like a fabric that can be warped."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891120,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can hold energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892080,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can even propagate waves—gravitational\nwaves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895680,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein showed that empty space has properties,\nso it must be real, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902160,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, maybe - but Einstein’s view is really\na radical departure from Newton’s—to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908760,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extent that Einstein even called himself a\nLeibnizian."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912900,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Newton believed in space as an underlying\nstage on which the particles and the fields"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919560,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "danced."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920520,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Einstein insisted that no such background\nexisted—and that’s because to him, space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925260,
    "dDurationMs": 2875,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the gravitational field are the same thing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928135,
    "dDurationMs": 3365,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This field is not painted on top \nof a coordinate system; rather,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931500,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the coordinate system is a quality of the field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934320,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Absent this field there is nothing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937740,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So all of this landed Einstein somewhere between\nLeibniz and Newton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942240,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He believed that there is an extended structure\n“out there” that can hold objects and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948180,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on which distances and durations can be defined,\nbut it’s not absolute and fundamental in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953460,
    "dDurationMs": 1080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way that Newton thought."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954540,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to Einstein, Descartes was right,\nand so was Plato: there’s no such thing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960180,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as empty space. To quote Einstein, \n\"there is no space empty of field\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966180,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is Einstein the last word on the matter?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970500,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Far from it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971940,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know that general relativity breaks down\non very small scales—smaller than around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976980,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10^-35 meters, which is the Planck length."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981420,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There it comes into hopeless conflict with\nquantum mechanics, and it becomes impossible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986700,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to meaningfully define shorter distances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989160,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just as it’s meaningless to define durations\nshorter than the Planck time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994560,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This conflict between Einstein’s theory\nand quantum mechanics is one of the major"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000140,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "challenges and inspirations for progressing\nto the next level of physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005600,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And essentially all of the possible paths\nforward force us to rethink our understanding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011420,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the dimensions—whether multiplying their\nnumber as in string theory, or by having them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017420,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emerge from elements that, themselves, do\nnot exist within space—such as in loop quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024380,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity, which we’ve discussed, or the cellular\nautomata of Wolframs physics project, or in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030980,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entanglements between \nelements on a holographic "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1033860,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon, or from Arkani-Hamed’s amplituhedron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037580,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "among others.."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038840,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If any of these latter are true, then Leibniz\nmay have been onto something; space exists"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046040,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the relationships between some sort of\nelementary…"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1050600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something, not as an absolute and physically\nreal fabric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055400,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Leibniz also had another controversial idea:\nhe thought that space was in our minds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062540,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This isn’t the same as saying that reality\nis in our minds—it’s not even the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066740,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as saying that space doesn’t exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1068600,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rather, Leibniz felt that whatever it is that’s\nout there that behaves like space only gains"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075380,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the subjective feeling of depth, breadth,\nheight, and distance when our brains try to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1080960,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "organise objects that are separated by an\naltogether more abstract property."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1086900,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kind of like how the subjective experience\nof red only exists when brains interpret a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092660,
    "dDurationMs": 840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequency of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093500,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s incredibly difficult to imagine a universe\nwithout space or time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097760,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The dimensions seem hardwired into our brains."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102140,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps we need to break this preconception\nto move forward in physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106160,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If so, we need to explore how and why our\nbrains build our very convincingly spatial"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1112820,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and temporal inner worlds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114380,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we’ll do that in an episode very soon,\nand perhaps get closer to figuring out whether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1120440,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we live in an absolute or a relational spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1125300,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS. \nBrilliant is an online learning platform for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130220,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "STEM with hands-on, interactive lessons. Brilliant \nis for curious learners, both young and old,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1135620,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "professional and inexperienced. Brilliant courses \nteach you how to think (via interactive lessons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141380,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and problem-solving activities/exercises.) and \nsolve problems with interactive lessons in STEM. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1148580,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, Artificial neural networks \nlearn by detecting patterns in huge amounts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153380,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of information. Like your own brain, \nartificial neural nets are flexible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1157460,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data-processing machines that make predictions \nand decisions. In fact, the best ones outperform  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1162440,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "humans at tasks like chess and cancer diagnoses.\nIn this course, you'll dissect the internal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1169100,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "machinery of artificial neural nets through \nhands-on experimentation, not hairy mathematics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1174620,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You'll develop intuition about the kinds \nof problems they are suited to solve,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1178580,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and by the end you’ll be ready to dive into \nthe algorithms, or build one for yourself. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1182420,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To learn more about Brilliant, \ngo to brilliant.org/spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1186680,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re looking at your comments from \nthe last two episodes. There was the one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1190580,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about how Earth really moves through the \nuniverse, and then the one about how the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1194660,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nucleus is held together by meson exchange. \nStarting with the motion of the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1200480,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Matt Thomas asks, when we put together all of \nour motion through the universe, how fast are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1205700,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we moving relative to the CMB? And what effect \ndoes that motion have on our experience of time?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1212720,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is that we’re moving at 368km/s \nrelative to the CMB. This isn’t unusual—most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1219860,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things in the universe have some relative \nvelocity like this. But you’re right that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1223880,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there should be a time dilation relative to the \nCMB. Let’s assume the frame of reference of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1230900,
    "dDurationMs": 8340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stuff of the Earth has on average been moving \nat that speed over the history of the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1239900,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Less time has passed in that reference frame \ncompared to the rest frame of the CMB—the Big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1247100,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bang was more recent for our hypothetical moving \nframe. I figured it out—the difference is about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1253820,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10,000 years. Pretty tiny compared \nto the age of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1257480,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Karl Sheffield asks what is in \nfront of our path around the Galaxy?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1263480,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, immediately in front: the interstellar \nmedium. The Sun’s heliosphere—a bubble  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1269600,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "containing its outward-flowing solar wind and \nmagnetic field—is plowing its way through very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1274220,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "low-density gas and dust grains. There \nare also bigger things that we can’t see  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1279020,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easily—bits of rock or ice like oumuamua \nthat were ejected from other star systems.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1285020,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There will be ejected planets, brown dwarfs, \nblack holes and other stellar remnants. In  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1291140,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "terms of stuff we can see—well we’re heading \nin the direction of the star Vega, but Vega is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1295400,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also orbiting the galaxy and so we’re not going \nto collide. That said, we do occasionally get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1300560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "close enough to a star or stellar remnant to mess \nwith orbits in our system, with the main danger  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1306560,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "being an increase in inner-solar system comets. \nThat’s more likely when we’re passing through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1312260,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the disk and especially in a spiral arm. It’ll \nbe millions of years before that happens again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1318440,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Moving on to the episode on \nthe strong nuclear force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1322160,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fensox asks whether Hideki Yukawa eventually got \nthe recognition he deserves for discovery of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1328460,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strong and weak forces. He did. He got the \n1949 Nobel Prize for predicting the existence  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1335120,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the pi meson. And his name is all over the \nstandard model—the Yukawa interaction governs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1341420,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strong force part of the standard model \nLagrangian as well the Higgs coupling term."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1345980,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Several people asked how it is that the \nexchange of virtual particles can cause  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1351380,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles to be attracted. After all, in \nthe analogy of particles throwing balls  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1356300,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at each other, it seems that the exchange \nof momentum should only push them apart.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1361460,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The short answer is that the balls analogy \nis a pretty limited one, and even the notion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1366800,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of virtual particles is something of a metaphor. \nWhat’s really happening is that the quantum fields  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1372500,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between and around the particles are disturbed in \na way that can be approximated as the work of many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1378140,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "virtual particles. But those virtual particles \ndon’t simply originate at particle one and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1384140,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "travel in a straight line to particle two. They \ncan originate in a wide region governed by the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1389660,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty principle, including on the opposite \nside of particle two. They can also have any mass,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1395060,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "including complex masses. All of this enables \nthe virtual particles to transfer momentum in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1400640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way that pulls the particles together instead \nof apart. But really, these particles are a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1406400,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematical fiction to describe field \nbehavior. No balls are being thrown."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1412640,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Feelincrispy points out that I could easily  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1414980,
    "dDurationMs": 7980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just make something up and %99.9 of \nyou would have no idea. I don't know if I agree with that, but otherwise I have no comment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1422960,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sleekweasel asks how the \nisland of stability works,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1426320,
    "dDurationMs": 5407,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "given that if a nucleus grows too big, \nits mesons can't hold it together.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1431727,
    "dDurationMs": 5933,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To remind everyone —the island of stability is \na region of the periodic table of very large  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1437660,
    "dDurationMs": 8971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclei that is theoretically more stable \nthan the current heavy end of the table that we've discovered at this point.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1446631,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, I don’t really know the details of this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1450301,
    "dDurationMs": 5539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But fortunately Gareth Dean jumped \nin to the comment section to answer, so I’m just going to read that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1455840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He says: Nuclei aren't just blobs of particles, \nthey have 'nuclear shells'. When these are empty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1461840,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the few particles in them are far apart and \ncannot exchange mesons. When they are full,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1468020,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lots of particles are packed close and can \nbind strongly. 'Islands of stability' are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1473600,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "places where the shells are full, binding \nis strong and the nucleus is more stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1478760,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regarding my use of the labradoodle to \nillustrate the amount of force between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1483680,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "adjacent protons in an atomic nucleus. Many of \nyou expressed interest in using labradoodles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1490100,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as some sort of standard unit of measurement. \nThis is a little impractical because we’d need  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1496040,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to use the mean weight of a statistically large number of labradoodles. But I personally volunteer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1502100,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to run the NIST labradoodle standards facility to \nmake sure those very good boys and girls get all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1507740,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their standard treats and pets. Many of you also \npointed out that a universe without labradoodles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1513680,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is not a universe they’d want to live in. Also \nagreed. Which brings us to Steve. Steve sees  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1522200,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the elimination of the strong nuclear force and \nwith it the elimination of all chemistry, biology  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1526700,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and life, as a promising way to rid the world of \nlabradoodles. Steve, you’ve identified yourself as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1534080,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "labradoodle-foe, and your name has been passed \nto a secret elite team at the NIST labradoodle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1539660,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "standards facility. They’ll be watching \nyou. In fact all labradoodles will be watching you."
    } ]
  } ]
}
